<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eamont Bridge</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eamont Bridge is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penrith">Penrith</a> on the River Eamont. On 12 July 927 the English king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> received there the submission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-ii-of-scotland">Constantine II of Scotland</a>, Owain of Strathclyde and other northern rulers, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>. The meeting is often taken as the moment when a single kingdom of England was first established.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eamont-bridge.jpg" alt="Eamont Bridge" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Northernhenge · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eamont_Bridge,_Cumbria.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
